  "grammarNotes": [
    {
      "id": "zahlen-0-20",
      "title": "Numbers 0–20",
      "body": "**elf** and **zwölf** are their own words. From 13 to 19, say the number and add **-zehn**: *drei + zehn = dreizehn*.\n\nTwo shortcuts: **sechzehn** drops the *s* of *sechs*, and **siebzehn** drops the *en* of *sieben*.",
      "table": [
        ["0–6", "7–13", "14–20"],
        ["0 null", "7 sieben", "14 vierzehn"],
        ["1 eins", "8 acht", "15 fünfzehn"],
        ["2 zwei", "9 neun", "16 sechzehn"],
        ["3 drei", "10 zehn", "17 siebzehn"],
        ["4 vier", "11 elf", "18 achtzehn"],
        ["5 fünf", "12 zwölf", "19 neunzehn"],
        ["6 sechs", "", "20 zwanzig"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "zahlen-tens",
      "title": "Tens and 21–99",
      "body": "Tens end in **-zig**, except **dreißig**, which ends in *-ßig*. As with the teens, **sechzig** and **siebzig** are shortened.\n\nFrom 21 to 99, German says the **ones first**, then **und**, then the tens, all written as one word:\n\n- *einundzwanzig* = one-and-twenty = 21\n- *fünfunddreißig* = five-and-thirty = 35\n- *neunundneunzig* = 99\n\n*eins* loses its *s* in front of *und*: **ein**undzwanzig.",
      "table": [
        ["Number", "German"],
        ["20", "zwanzig"],
        ["30", "dreißig"],
        ["40", "vierzig"],
        ["50", "fünfzig"],
        ["60", "sechzig"],
        ["70", "siebzig"],
        ["80", "achtzig"],
        ["90", "neunzig"],
        ["100", "hundert / einhundert"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "zahlen-age",
      "title": "Saying your age",
      "body": "German uses *sein* (to be) for age, just like English:\n\n- **Wie alt bist du?** / **Wie alt sind Sie?** How old are you?\n- **Ich bin 25 Jahre alt.** or simply **Ich bin 25.**\n\nUse **Jahre** (years) for every age except one: *ein Jahr alt*."
    },
    {
      "id": "zahlen-phone",
      "title": "Phone numbers",
      "body": "Germans read phone numbers digit by digit or in pairs: *0171 2468* is *null eins sieben eins, zwo vier sechs acht* or *… vierundzwanzig, achtundsechzig*.\n\nOn the phone, many people say **zwo** instead of *zwei* so it can't be confused with *drei*.\n\nDidn't catch it? Say **Wie bitte?** (Pardon?) or **Noch einmal, bitte.** (Once more, please.)"
    }
  ],
